  "source": {
    "title": "Lurgan Mail, 6 January 1923, p. 5 (advertisement \"Lurgan Municipal Election, January 1923\": the fifteen candidates adopted by the Joint Committee of the Orange Institution and other local Unionist organisations); Lurgan Mail, 13 January 1923, p. 5 (\"Notes and Notions\": \"the unchallenged return of fifteen Unionist candidates\")",
    "publisher": "Lurgan Mail (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "names read from the page image of the 6 January advertisement; the 13 January editorial confirms that the fifteen were returned unopposed"
  },
  "note": "First election after proportional representation was abolished (Local Government Act (Northern Ireland) 1922). Lurgan was not divided into wards: the whole town returned fifteen councillors (Ministry of Home Affairs order of 11 November 1922, Belfast Gazette, 17 November 1922). Nominations closed on 5 January 1923 and no more than fifteen remained, so there was no poll on 15 January. By a further order of 13 November 1922 Lurgan returned to annual election by thirds: five of these fifteen were to retire in January 1924, five in 1925 and five in 1926. All fifteen were the Unionist organisations' nominees; the Lurgan Mail regretted that no member of the minority was included. The name is printed \"Carrick\" here; other reports print \"Garrick\"."
}
